Only problem with an early return is that `mHeldTransactions` gets reset with the `parentHash` at the end of this function, which is used to salt the account ID, and thus affects the ordering of later held transactions. I wasn't explicit that my thinking was that the `openLedger().modify` would be wrapped in `if (mHeldTransactions.size()) {}`.

To be fair, I don't know how important the ordering is in the context of held transactions, but in the absence of other information, I have to assume it was done intentionally.

Another option is:
```
void
LedgerMaster::applyHeldTransactions()
{
    auto const held = [&]() {
        std::lock_guard sl(m_mutex);
        auto const result = mHeldTransactions;

        // VFALCO TODO recreate the CanonicalTxSet object instead of resetting
        // it.
        // VFALCO NOTE The hash for an open ledger is undefined so we use
        // something that is a reasonable substitute.
        mHeldTransactions.reset(app_.openLedger().current()->info().parentHash);

        return result;
    }();
    if (!held.size())
        return;

    app_.openLedger().modify([&](OpenView& view, beast::Journal j) {
        bool any = false;
        for (auto const& it : held)
        {
            ApplyFlags flags = tapNONE;
            auto const result =
                app_.getTxQ().apply(app_, view, it.second, flags, j);
            if (result.second)
                any = true;
        }
        return any;
    });
}
```
`OpenLedger` has it's own lock, so this should be safe, though I haven't tested it or anything.

I'm not too keen on the double `unlock(); setBatchApplyTimer()` calls.  I think the following restructure is simpler and equivalent to what you have:
```
void
NetworkOPsImp::setBatchApplyTimer()
{
    using namespace std::chrono_literals;
    auto constexpr batchInterval = 100ms;

    setTimer(
        batchApplyTimer_,
        batchInterval,
        [this]() {
            {
                std::lock_guard lock(mMutex);
                // Only do the job if there's work to do and it's not currently
                // being done.
                if (mTransactions.size() &&
                    mDispatchState == DispatchState::none)
                {
                    if (m_job_queue.addJob(
                            jtBATCH, "transactionBatch", [this]() {
                                transactionBatch(false);
                            }))
                    {
                        mDispatchState = DispatchState::scheduled;
                    }
                    return;
                }
            }
            setBatchApplyTimer();
        },
        [this]() { setBatchApplyTimer(); });
}
```
